{
  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "PARTIAL_REFUND",
    "amount_sgd": 13.70,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.9,
    "explanation": "Both parties are, on the evidence, largely agreed, and this ruling follows the policy arithmetic.\n\n1. Did the route deviate? Yes. POL-RD-01 makes the GPS trace the governing measurement, not the trip record's rounded copy: the trace between the recorded pickup and dropoff is 23.97 km against the booked optimal of 18.0 km, an excess of 5.97 km, or 33.17% of optimal. Both establishment tests (more than 20% of optimal and more than 0.8 km) pass, so the deviation is ESTABLISHED. It is not a MATERIAL deviation: 5.97 km is under both the 50% and the 8.0 km thresholds, so no POL-RD-06 driver-profile check is engaged, no FULL_REFUND uplift is available, and no driver-conduct referral arises.\n\n2. Was it excused? No. POL-RD-03 needs a traffic advisory naming an expressway segment the optimal route uses, with an overlapping validity window and a matching detour. There is none. The only advisory artifact, EV-ADVIS-00114-01, is MISSING with weight 0.0 and issue code MISSING_ARTIFACT; it can carry no fact and is cited only for its absence, which is why it neither drags the confidence below the floor nor requires escalation. The driver's congestion account cites no advisory artifact, and POL-RD-08 puts the burden on the driver to point to a specific artifact and clause; a general assertion that traffic was bad does not discharge it. The driver himself says he knows of no advisory. His own record also shows the route was slower, not faster (40 minutes actual against the 30-minute optimal). There was no rider-requested detour under POL-RD-04 (the rider's chat message was an objection to the Adam Road route, not a request, and both waypoint hints are null), no pickup or dropoff relocation under POL-RD-05, and this was a solo RYDE_X trip, so POL-RD-12 does not apply.\n\n3. The money, under POL-RD-02 (the fare model is METERED):\n- charged_total: SGD 58.10\n- recomputed_fare: ((3.80 base + 0.75 x 18.0 km + 0.28 x 30.0 min) x 1.6 surge read from fare_breakdown) + 0.80 platform fee + 2.50 on-route ERP = (25.70 x 1.6) + 0.80 + 2.50 = 44.42\n- refund: 58.10 - 44.42 = 13.68, rounded up to SGD 13.70.\nThe 1.6 multiplier is read from fare_breakdown.surge_multiplier, as the clause requires and as the rider accepts; it is applied to the recomputed optimal fare, which is why the refund is partial rather than the whole fare difference. The result is above the 0.50 de-minimis floor and is 23.6% of the charge, well short of the 90% that would make it a full refund.\n\n4. ERP. The two lines total 4.50. The Kim Keat Link gantry (2.50) lies on the optimal route and is inside the recomputed fare; the Adam Road gantry (2.00) is off the optimal route and is already comprehended in the 13.70 refund. POL-RD-09(1) requires the off-route line to be itemised for transparency but forbids adding it on top, so the rider recovers it once and not twice. The rider's suggestion that the whole 4.50 was avoidable is not correct: only the Adam Road pass was off the optimal route. Both gantries are real crossings (nearest fixes 0.0 m and 8.76 m, inside the 250 m radius), so there is no billing-integrity defect and no referral under POL-RD-09(4).\n\n5. No escalation. The trip identifiers match across the three core artifacts, the trace is unimpaired under POL-RD-07 (241 fixes at a 10-second interval, no gaps, no jumps), the timelines are consistent, and the refund is far below the SGD 150 ceiling. The record is complete enough to decide.\n\nOutcome: PARTIAL_REFUND of SGD 13.70 to the rider. The driver is owed no compensation and no referral is made."
  },
